\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Component ablation on Qwen-2.5-7B ($N{=}1{,}190$). Tier~1 signals achieve near-perfect accuracy; combining them (VOTA) slightly dilutes performance. Entries marked $^\dag  $/$^\S  $/$^\ddag  $ are 3-seed mean$\pm $std; per-seed breakdowns in Appendix~\ref {sec:full_results}. Seed $\sigma $ is lowest for \textit  {exception\_only} ($\sigma {=}0.00$) and highest for \textit  {loop\_only} ($\sigma {=}0.52$); \textit  {ae\_only} ($\sigma {=}0.49$) is an outlier, suggesting seed sensitivity tracks input length rather than signal density alone.}}{4}{table.caption.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:ablation}{{2}{4}{Component ablation on Qwen-2.5-7B ($N{=}1{,}190$). Tier~1 signals achieve near-perfect accuracy; combining them (VOTA) slightly dilutes performance. Entries marked $^\dag $/$^\S $/$^\ddag $ are 3-seed mean$\pm $std; per-seed breakdowns in Appendix~\ref {sec:full_results}. Seed $\sigma $ is lowest for \cond {exception\_only} ($\sigma {=}0.00$) and highest for \cond {loop\_only} ($\sigma {=}0.52$); \cond {ae\_only} ($\sigma {=}0.49$) is an outlier, suggesting seed sensitivity tracks input length rather than signal density alone}{table.caption.3}{}}
